body.proof_tex:
All upper and lower bounds below use the same law--function class \(\mathcal M_n\) and the same decision class \(\mathcal T_n\).  The identifying equality \(\tau(P)=E_P[g_1(X)-g_0(X)]\) is part of the frozen target; the estimators below are sample maps and are not definitions of that causal target.

First, \texttt{thm:clipped-absolute-upper} states that \(\widehat\tau_{\lambda_n^{\star}}\in\mathcal T_n\) and, on the broader class with the two richness assumptions deleted, has mean absolute error at most \(C\rho_n\).  Restricting its supremum to \(\mathcal M_n\) and applying \texttt{def:absolute-risk} therefore gives
\[
\mathfrak R_n^{(1)}
\leq
\sup_{(P,m_0,m_1,p)\in\mathcal M_n}
E_P\!\left|\widehat\tau_{\lambda_n^{\star}}-\tau(P)\right|
\leq C\rho_n.                                                    \tag{71}
\]

We next prove the root-mean-squared upper bound.  The proof of \texttt{thm:clipped-absolute-upper}, before its choice of threshold, establishes for an i.i.d. sample of any size \(N\geq1\) and any \(0<\lambda\leq\lambda_0\) the finite-threshold inequality
\[
\begin{split}
E_P|\widehat\tau_{\lambda,N}-\tau(P)|
\leq C\,U_N(\lambda),\qquad
U_N(\lambda):={}&
\frac{v_\kappa(\lambda)}{\sqrt N}
+\frac{r_{1n}}{\sqrt{N\lambda}}
+\frac{r_{en}}{\sqrt N\,\lambda^{3/2}}\\
&+r_{1n}\lambda^{\kappa/2}
+\frac{r_{1n}r_{en}}{\lambda}
+r_{0n}r_{en}.                                             \tag{72}
\end{split}
\]
Here the subscript \(N\) records only the number of observations averaged; the public certificates remain the supplied \(r_{0n},r_{1n},r_{en}\).  Inequality (72) follows there from \texttt{ass:iid}, the uniform shell calculus, the right-overlap bound, the two public regression certificates, the public propensity certificate, the treatment-effect signal moment bound, and the two conditional residual-variance bounds.  Thus it applies directly to every block; membership in a separately defined \(\mathcal M_N\) is not required.

For \(n\geq5\), write \(n=5q+r\), where \(q\geq1\) and \(0\leq r\leq4\).  A block size \(N_j\) is either \(q\) or \(q+1\), while \(n=5q+r\leq5q+4\leq9q\).  Hence
\[
N_j\geq n/9.
\]
Only the first three terms of \(U_N(\lambda)\) depend on \(N\), and all three are proportional to \(N^{-1/2}\).  Consequently
\[
U_{N_j}(\lambda_n^{\star})\leq3U_n(\lambda_n^{\star}).     \tag{73}
\]
The threshold optimization and the cap-binding case analysis in \texttt{thm:clipped-absolute-upper}, using \texttt{def:oracle-threshold}, \texttt{def:tail-noise-rate}, and \texttt{def:sharp-rate}, establish
\[
U_n(\lambda_n^{\star})\leq C\rho_n.                       \tag{74}
\]
Equations (72)--(74) therefore yield a constant \(K<\infty\), uniform in \(n\), the admissible radii, and \((P,m_0,m_1,p)\in\mathcal M_n\), such that
\[
E_P\!\left|\widehat\tau_{\lambda_n^{\star}}^{(j)}-\tau(P)\right|
\leq K\rho_n,
\qquad 1\leq j\leq5.                                    \tag{75}
\]
The block estimators are independent by \texttt{ass:iid}.  Applying \texttt{lem:median-risk-upgrade} to (75) gives
\[
\sup_{(P,m_0,m_1,p)\in\mathcal M_n}
\left\{E_P\!\left[(\widehat\tau_{\mathrm{med}}-\tau(P))^2\right]\right\}^{1/2}
\leq\sqrt{21}\,K\rho_n                                  \tag{76}
\]
for \(n\geq5\).

It remains to verify the full-sample fallback for \(1\leq n<5\) without imposing a separate second-moment bound on \(g_0\), \(g_1\), or \(Y\).  Since \(n\leq4\), \texttt{def:oracle-threshold} gives
\[
\lambda_n^{\star}\geq
\lambda_{\min}:=\min\{\lambda_0,4^{-1/(\kappa+1)}\}>0,
\qquad 1-p^{\circ}(X)\geq\eta_+/2.                       \tag{77}
\]
Define \(\Delta=g_1-g_0\), \(h_a=m_a-g_a\), and
\(\varepsilon_a=Y-g_a(X)\) on \(\{D=a\}\).  Direct substitution into \texttt{def:clipped-score} gives the exact identity
\[
\psi_{\lambda_n^{\star}}
=\Delta+h_1-h_0
+\frac{D(\varepsilon_1-h_1)}{q_{\lambda_n^{\star}}}
-\frac{(1-D)(\varepsilon_0-h_0)}{1-p^{\circ}}.             \tag{78}
\]
All denominators in (78) are bounded away from zero by (77).  By the treatment-effect signal moment assumption and the public regression certificates,
\[
\|\Delta\|_{P,2}\leq\sqrt M,
\qquad \|h_a\|_{P,2}\leq r_{an}.
\]
The conditional residual-variance assumptions, \((x-y)^2\leq2x^2+2y^2\), and (77) imply
\[
E_P\!\left[
\frac{D(\varepsilon_1-h_1)^2}{q_{\lambda_n^{\star}}^2}
\right]
\leq\frac{2(\sigma_1^2+r_{1n}^2)}{\lambda_{\min}^2},
\]
and
\[
E_P\!\left[
\frac{(1-D)(\varepsilon_0-h_0)^2}{(1-p^{\circ})^2}
\right]
\leq\frac{8(\sigma_0^2+r_{0n}^2)}{\eta_+^2}.              \tag{79}
\]
For example, the first inequality uses
\(E_P[D\varepsilon_1^2]=E_P[e(X)E_P(\varepsilon_1^2\mid D=1,X)]\leq\sigma_1^2\) and \(E_P[Dh_1^2]\leq r_{1n}^2\); the control calculation is analogous.  Minkowski's inequality in (78), (79), and the radius cap \(r_{an}\leq\bar r\) now give
\[
\begin{split}
\|\psi_{\lambda_n^{\star}}\|_{P,2}
\leq{}&\sqrt M+r_{1n}+r_{0n}
+\lambda_{\min}^{-1}\sqrt{2(\sigma_1^2+r_{1n}^2)}\\
&+\frac{2}{\eta_+}\sqrt{2(\sigma_0^2+r_{0n}^2)}
\leq C_0.                                                  \tag{80}
\end{split}
\]
Also, Cauchy--Schwarz gives
\(|\tau(P)|=|E_P\Delta(X)|\leq\|\Delta\|_{P,2}\leq\sqrt M\).  Hence \(\|\psi_{\lambda_n^{\star}}-\tau(P)\|_{P,2}\leq C_1\).  Pointwise convexity of the square yields
\[
\begin{split}
E_P\!\left[(\widehat\tau_{\lambda_n^{\star}}-\tau(P))^2\right]
&\leq\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n
E_P\!\left[(\psi_{\lambda_n^{\star}}(O_i)-\tau(P))^2\right]\\
&\leq C_1^2.                                               \tag{81}
\end{split}
\]
By \texttt{def:tail-noise-rate} and \texttt{def:sharp-rate}, \(\rho_n\geq n^{-1/2}\geq1/2\) for \(n<5\), so the last display is at most \((2C_1\rho_n)^2\).  Combining this fact with (76), the membership \(\widehat\tau_{\mathrm{med}}\in\mathcal T_n\) from \texttt{lem:median-risk-upgrade}, and the square-inside-the-expectation definition \texttt{def:root-mse-risk}, we obtain
\[
\mathfrak R_n^{(2)}\leq C\rho_n                           \tag{82}
\]
for every \(n\geq1\).

For a fixed \(\delta\in(0,1/4)\), the deviation conclusion of \texttt{thm:clipped-absolute-upper} supplies an admissible radius \(C\rho_n/\delta\) in \texttt{def:probability-risk}.  Therefore
\[
\mathfrak R_{n,\delta}^{(\mathrm{prob})}
\leq C_\delta\rho_n.                                     \tag{83}
\]

For the converse, let \(c>0\) and \(\delta_{*}>0\) be constants furnished by \texttt{thm:four-channel-converse}.  Because those constants are existential and independent of \(n\) and the radii, set \(\delta_0=\min\{\delta_{*}/2,1/8\}\).  The same converse then gives, for every \(T\in\mathcal T_n\),
\[
\sup_{(P,m_0,m_1,p)\in\mathcal M_n}
P\!\left\{|T-\tau(P)|\geq c\rho_n\right\}
\geq\delta_{*}\geq2\delta_0.                             \tag{84}
\]
It also gives directly
\[
\mathfrak R_n^{(1)}\geq c_1\rho_n,
\qquad
\mathfrak R_n^{(2)}\geq c_2\rho_n                         \tag{85}
\]
for positive constants \(c_1,c_2\) depending only on the fixed class constants.  If \(0<\delta\leq\delta_0\) and \(0\leq u<c\rho_n\), then (84) implies
\[
\sup_{(P,m_0,m_1,p)\in\mathcal M_n}
P\{|T-\tau(P)|>u\}
\geq2\delta_0>\delta.                                    \tag{86}
\]
Thus no \(u<c\rho_n\) belongs to the inner feasible set in \texttt{def:probability-risk}.  Taking its infimum and then the infimum over \(T\in\mathcal T_n\) proves
\[
\mathfrak R_{n,\delta}^{(\mathrm{prob})}\geq c\rho_n.     \tag{87}
\]

Equations (71), (82), (83), (85), and (87) establish all three asserted equivalences.  The constants are uniform over the bounded admissible radius triples and the fixed reservoir-rich shell constants; in (83) the upper constant may additionally depend on the fixed \(\delta\).  The lower bounds use the reservoir-rich class \(\mathcal M_n\), whereas the clipped-estimator argument, its blockwise repetition, and median amplification remain valid on the broader uniform one-sided-shell class obtained by deleting the two richness assumptions.
